<h1 id="Final-Conclusion">Final Conclusion<a class="anchor-link" href="#Final-Conclusion">¶</a></h1><p>In this assignment, we built and evaluated GLM models (Lasso, Ridge, ElasticNet) for predicting loan default risks on an imbalanced dataset.</p>
<ul>
<li>L1 Logistic Regression (Lasso) achieved the best recall (67.11%) and strong ROC AUC (~0.7460) among GLMs.</li>
<li>L2 Logistic Regression (Ridge) showed moderate performance with ROC AUC ~0.6242.</li>
<li>ElasticNet performed the weakest among the three GLMs (~0.5992 ROC AUC).</li>
</ul>
<p>Each model was evaluated using Accuracy, Precision, Recall, F1 Score, ROC AUC, and Cumulative Lift charts. We also visualized the top 15 most important features influencing model predictions. Ensemble models from Assignment-1 (Random Forest, GBM, XGBoost, CatBoost) were compared.</p>
<ul>
<li>CatBoost achieved the highest ROC AUC (0.7601), making it the best overall model.</li>
<li>Lasso Logistic Regression was the best among GLMs for simplicity and strong recall.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Final Recommendations:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Use CatBoost for maximum predictive power.</li>
<li>Use Lasso Logistic Regression for simple, interpretable, and high-recall scenarios.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Additional Insights:</strong></p>
<p>Through this assignment, it became clear that while traditional GLM models can achieve good performance with appropriate regularization and handling of class imbalance, ensemble techniques like CatBoost consistently offer superior results for complex, high-stakes problems like credit risk prediction. Moreover, the careful evaluation using multiple performance metrics and the use of lift charts provided a deeper understanding of model behavior beyond mere accuracy. This structured and multi-faceted approach to model selection is essential for designing reliable, production-ready solutions in real-world financial risk environments.</p>
